# Why is water cheap and diamonds expensive?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why is something essential so cheap and something useless so dear? How did thinking at the margin solve the paradox that puzzled Adam Smith?"
next: {"title":"Where does a price come from if nobody sets it?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/07-where-does-a-price-come-from-if-nobody.cast"}

## Smith's puzzle
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - value in use
      - value in exchange
    series:
      - {name: diamond, values: [1.5, 9]}
      - {name: water, values: [10, 0.4]}
    y_ticks: false
    y_label: how much
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 11
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 480
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: smith1776
      title: An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, Book I, ch. IV–V
      authors: Adam Smith
      year: 1776
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Smith/smWN.html
      finding: 'Value in use vs value in exchange: ''Nothing is more useful than water: but it will purchase scarce any thing''; the real price of every thing is ''the toil and trouble of acquiring it'''

    icon water_icon size 110 x 780 y 470 or ["droplet"] icon_look picture of "glass of water" hidden true
    icon gem_icon size 110 x 910 y 470 or ["diamond"] icon_look picture of "gem stone" hidden true

    card title "Why is water cheap and diamonds expensive?"

Why is water, which keeps you alive, nearly free — while a di (@draw gem_icon@)amond, which keeps nobody alive, costs a fortune?
    draw water_icon

Score them on two things: how useful each is, and what it sells for.
    draw axes legend

On use, water wins by miles.
    draw bar_1

On price, the diamond wins by miles.
    draw bar_2

    pause 0.4

B: Isn't that just vanity? Diamonds are a status thing.
    highlight gem_icon

Some of it, maybe. But bread is cheaper than perfume and salt cheaper than silk — vanity can't explain every flip.
    highlight bar_2

    erase water_icon gem_icon

The puzzle is old: Adam Smith set it out in 1776, in The Wealth of Nations.
    portrait smith of "Adam Smith" cameo true

    erase smith

'Nothing is more useful than water,' he wrote, 'but it will purchase scarce any thing.'
    source wn cites ["smith1776"] x 840 y 420 of "The Wealth of Nations"

He split value in two: value in use and value in exchange — and they seemed to point in opposite directions.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    erase wn

Smith's own answer was labour: the real price of a thing is the toil and trouble of getting it.
    text labour "price = toil and trouble?" cites ["smith1776"] x 830 y 330 font_size 30

B: So diamonds are dear because digging them out is hard work?
    highlight labour

That is part of it. But a diamond found by luck in a riverbed sells for no less than one dug out of a mine.
    fade labour to 0.35

So the puzzle stands: why does price follow the rare thing, not the useful one?
    highlight bar_2

    quiz "Why does a diamond cost more than a glass of water?"
        right: How much of each we already have decides what one more is worth — and that is what the price follows.
        wrong: Think about the tenth glass of water you drink today, not the first.
        * Diamonds take more work to dig out
        * People are vain about diamonds
        + It depends on how much of each we already have

## The last glass
domain: {"x":[0,20],"y":[0,105]}
vars:
    Q:
      value: 16
      min: 1
      max: 20
    mv:
      expr: 100/(1+0.5*Q)
sources:
    - id: marginal1871
      title: The Theory of Political Economy (Jevons, 1871); Grundsätze der Volkswirthschaftslehre (Menger, 1871); Éléments d'économie politique pure (Walras, 1874)
      authors: W. S. Jevons; C. Menger; L. Walras
      year: 1871
      finding: 'Independently founded marginal utility theory: value depends on the utility of the last unit, resolving the water–diamond paradox'

    axes ax x_label "units you have (Q)" y_label "value of one more" cross corner hidden true
    curve base expr "0" style.opacity 0 hidden true
    curve water thick #2f6b8f expr 100/(1+0.5*x) x_from 0 x_to 20 hidden true
    dot p1 #2f6b8f at.on water at.x 0.15 hidden true
    label l_p1 "first glass" right #2f6b8f attach_to p1 hidden true
    label l_mv "water: marginal value" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to water hidden true
    region total x_from 0 x_to.ref pw between ["water","base"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text l_total "total value" at.data [6,10] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot pw #2f6b8f at.on water at.x 16 bind {"at.x":"Q"} hidden true
    label l_pw "last glass: {mv:0}" above #2f6b8f attach_to pw hidden true
    curve diamond thick #b5482e expr 50*exp(-x/1.2) x_from 0 x_to 6 hidden true
    dot pd #b5482e at.on diamond at.x 0.3 hidden true
    label l_pd "diamond: your first" right #b5482e attach_to pd hidden true
    portrait jevons of "William Stanley Jevons" cameo true hidden true
    scratch mathcard top-right work [{"tex":"MU = \\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta Q}"},{"tex":"\\text{buy until } MU = P"},{"tex":"\\frac{P_{water}}{P_{diamond}} = \\frac{MU_{water}}{MU_{diamond}}"}] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The last glass"

B: So which is it — is water worth a lot, or almost nothing?
    draw ax base

Both — and a drawing shows how. Across, the glasses of water you have today; up, what one more glass is worth to you.
    highlight ax

The first glass, when you are parched, is worth almost anything.
    draw p1 l_p1

The second a bit less; the tenth is for washing; the twentieth waters the plants.
    draw water

    erase l_p1

B: So water loses its value the more you drink?
    highlight water

Not water — one more glass. Economists call that marginal value: marginal just means the next one.
    draw l_mv

All the glasses together, this whole area, are worth an enormous amount to you.
    draw total l_total

But you never buy all of them at once. You buy the  (@draw l_pw@)next one, and with plenty on tap the next one is worth almost nothing.
    draw pw

    pause 0.5

B: And diamonds? I'd guess their curve is simply higher.
    highlight water

    fade total l_total to 0.3

Lower, in fact: diamonds add little to anyone's life in total.
    draw diamond

But most of us own none, so we sit at the very start of the curve, where one more is worth a lot.
    draw pd l_pd

Three economists — Jevons and Menger in 1871, Walras in 1874 — reached this separately: price follows the value of the last unit, not of all of them.
    draw jevons

    erase jevons

Try it: drag the glasses Harbourtown has down, as in a drought, and watch the last glass climb.
    animate Q 6 duration 3

    explore params ["Q"]

    animate Q 16 duration 1.5

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Want the math behind the last glass?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

In symbols: marginal utility, MU, is the extra utility U from one more unit.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

You keep buying until the last unit is worth just its price — MU equals P, with utility counted in money.
    draw mathcard_line_2

So prices line up with the value of the last units: water's price over a diamond's is a tiny MU over a large one.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    fade total l_total to 1

B: So water is cheap because we have so much of it — not because it's useless.
    highlight total pw

## Two blades
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Quantity (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      label: marginal value
    supply:
      label: S
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      shift_label: ''
      shift_p_label: P
      shift_q_label: Q
    supply_shift:
      amount: 35
      label: marginal cost
      arrow: horizontal
sources:
    - id: marshall1890
      title: Principles of Economics, Book V, ch. III
      authors: Alfred Marshall
      year: 1890
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Marshall/marP30.html
      finding: '''We might as reasonably dispute whether it is the upper or the under blade of a pair of scissors that cuts a piece of paper, as whether value is governed by utility or cost of production.'''
    - id: epstein1982
      title: Have You Ever Tried to Sell a Diamond?
      authors: Edward Jay Epstein, The Atlantic
      year: 1982
      finding: De Beers controlled most of the world's rough-diamond supply for much of the twentieth century and stockpiled stones to hold prices up

    portrait marshall of "Alfred Marshall" cameo true hidden true
    text sure "consensus" #87a878 at.percent [84,88] cites ["marshall1890"] font_size 36 hidden true
    mark sure_box around sure kind box hidden true
    label debeers "held back" left #b5482e attach_to supply_shift_curve cites ["epstein1982"] hidden true

    card title "Two blades"

    hide supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar supply_shift_arrow

B: The last glass solved half the puzzle. It left out the other half.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D

Which half? Surely value is just what people want.
    highlight demand_curve

B: Smith's labour story never went away. Cost decides how much gets supplied — and so how far down the curve we end up.
    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

B: Water is cheap to pump, so a lot is supplied, and we end up far down, where the last glass is worth little.
    draw shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_P_shift label_Q_shift

B: So both matter, as Alfred Marshall put it in 1890: asking whether utility or cost sets value is like asking which blade of the scissors cuts the paper.
    draw marshall

    erase marshall

So diamonds sit where cost is high: few are dug, and the last one is still worth a lot.
    animate supply_shift.amount -28 duration 3 ghost true

But isn't some of that scarcity made?
    highlight supply_shift_curve

B: Some of it. For most of the twentieth century one firm, De Beers, controlled most of the world's rough dia (@draw debeers@)monds and held stones back.
    animate supply_shift.amount -38 duration 2.5

So the margin explains how the price works — not whether the scarcity behind it is natural.
    highlight shift_equilibrium_point label_P_shift

    erase debeers

B: How sure are we? That price tracks the value of the last unit, not the total, is about as close to consensus as economics gets.
    draw sure sure_box

B: What changed is the utility part: the marginalists hoped to measure it like weight; today's theory only needs people to rank their options.
    highlight label_D

    quiz "Harbourtown's reservoir runs low and water is rationed. What happens to what people would pay for one more litre?"
        right: It rises: with less water, the last litre goes to drinking, not the plants, so it is worth far more.
        wrong: Ask which litre you would give up: the one for the plants, or one you drink.
        + It rises sharply
        * It stays the same: water is just as useful
        * It falls

    quiz "Labs learn to grow diamonds identical to mined ones, cheaply and in any number. What happens to the price of a diamond?" voice b
        right: It falls: supply moves out, buyers move down the marginal-value curve, and the last stone is worth little more than it costs to grow.
        wrong: Think about how much the last buyer would pay once stones are plentiful.
        + It falls toward the cost of growing one
        * It stays high: diamonds are still beautiful
        * It rises: more people can now get one

    quiz "Air is more useful than water or diamonds, yet it is free. Why?"
        right: All the air is worth everything to us; one more breath, with air all around, is worth nothing — and price follows the last breath.
        wrong: Compare the value of all the air with the value of one more breath.
        * Air isn't very useful
        + One more breath is worth nothing when air is everywhere
        * The government sets its price at zero

So water is cheap not because it is worth little, but because we have so much that the last glass is worth (@highlight shift_equilibrium_point label_P_shift@) little — and how a market finds that price with nobody setting it is the next puzzle.
    animate supply_shift.amount 35 duration 3

